Score-view (\code{view = "scores"}) statuses distinguish \code{available_with_caveat},
\code{supporting_route}, and \code{unavailable}.

\strong{One-family vs two-family scope.} One-family \code{GPCM} estimates positive
relative discriminations (geometric mean \code{1}) on \code{slope_facet}; MML permits
\code{slope_facet != step_facet} and defaults to \code{gpcm_mml_identification = "free_population"} (estimating population SD \eqn{\sigma}, so standardized
unit-variance slopes are \eqn{a_i = \sigma\alpha_i}), whereas JML requires
\code{slope_facet == step_facet} and offers exploratory location SEs without
structural slope intervals. Provisional two-family \code{GPCM} requires fixed
\eqn{N(0,1)} MML (\code{"em"} fixed-grid or \code{"direct"} adaptive) on zero-based
unweighted integer scores (\code{0:rating_max}). When extreme responses occur on
one non-reference facet, \code{fit$point_limit} records any supported
limiting-likelihood refit (\code{PrimaryEstimate} vs working \code{OptimizerEstimate},
or \code{neutral_boundary_reference} when full optimization is skipped).
